# certifi arrives via requests (utils/graph.py, and google-auth's transport
# under google-cloud-storage and firebase-admin) and via httpx/httpcore ←
# firebase-admin, which builds no client here (see anyio); requests verifies
# against certifi.where(), and nothing sets REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE or
# CURL_CA_BUNDLE, the two variables that would override it. 2026.10.23
# closes the critical GHSA-6q48-6c7w-9gw2 (PYSEC-2026-4320, no CVE; fixed in
# 2026.10.18): certifi trusted emSign ECC Root CA - G3, under which eMudhra
# revoked eight intermediate CAs, six of them for TLS, whose private keys
# anyone can recompute from the public certificates (the secret scalars are
# tiny; CPython gh-160040), so anyone can sign a certificate for any host.
# Reachable in principle: an attacker on-path between App Engine and
# login.microsoftonline.com, graph.microsoft.com, storage.googleapis.com,
# iamcredentials.googleapis.com, www.googleapis.com (the keys that verify
# Firebase ID tokens) or identitytoolkit.googleapis.com could intercept
# those calls. 2026.10.23 is the latest release: against 2026.4.22 the store
# loses six roots (three emSign — ECC Root CA - G3, Root CA - C1, ECC Root
# CA - C3 — Atos TrustedRoot 2011, SecureSign Root CA12 and Chunghwa
# Telecom's ePKI Root Certification Authority) and gains four (SECOM TLS RSA
# and ECC Root CA 2024, Telia RSA and EC TLS Root CA v3). On 2026-11-05 a
# handshake from a workstation to each host above verified against it
# alone, every chain ending at GTS Root R4 or DigiCert Global Root G2, in
# both bundles.
# NOT covered by this pin: gRPC verifies against grpcio's own bundled roots
# (see grpcio below), and App Check's JWKS fetch (pyjwt's urllib) against the
# OS store.
certifi==2026.10.23
# grpcio carries every gRPC call (Firestore, Secret Manager, Cloud Tasks,
# Cloud Logging, the OTLP export) and verifies TLS against the roots bundled
# in its own wheel (grpc/_cython/_credentials/roots.pem): no channel here
# passes roots, and nothing sets GRPC_DEFAULT_SSL_ROOTS_FILE_PATH or
# GRPC_USE_SYSTEM_ROOTS_OVER_LANGUAGE_CALLBACK (either would move it off
# that bundle). 1.80.0's bundle (130 roots) still trusted emSign ECC Root
# CA - G3 (see certifi above) and AAA Certificate Services, Sectigo's
# cross-signer of the same eight intermediates (gRPC's own advisory,
# GHSA-2xm2-c8xx-p9vh, critical, 2026-10-27, sits in neither GitHub's global
# database nor OSV, so no alert flagged it). 1.80.1 (2026-11-05, grpc#43845)
# replaced it with Chromium's root store (99 roots), which drops both and
# keeps GTS Root R1 and R4, the roots Google's endpoints chain to; the 1.85
# line got the same store through grpc#43717 (2026-10-23), before 1.85.0,
# and 1.85.1's roots.pem is byte-identical to 1.80.1's. Reachable in
# principle like certifi's, though the path from App Engine to
# *.googleapis.com stays inside Google's network. On 2026-11-06 a channel
# through 1.85.1's own roots reached READY from a workstation to every
# endpoint the app dials (firestore, secretmanager, cloudtasks, logging and
# telemetry.googleapis.com), while a store missing Google's roots failed;
# since 1.83.0 the handshake offers X25519MLKEM768 first.
# 1.80.1 -> 1.85.1 (2026-11-06) also brings the fixes of gRPC's
# GHSA-hf3w-6hpw-qp67 (high, 2026-08-28, <= 1.83.0, in no database either):
# memory exhaustion from a peer's GOAWAY frame, floods of tiny DATA frames
# or a decompressed message past the receive cap. Only a peer that passes
# TLS as *.googleapis.com talks to these channels, so a hygiene bump; an
# oversized message now fails RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED in the transport, and the
# google-cloud clients lift the cap (-1 both ways). GRPC_ENABLE_FORK_SUPPORT
# still defaults to off, and gunicorn forks its workers before they import
# grpc. Its Python fixes (a Health Watch DoS on gRPC servers, an asyncio
# interceptor leak) touch nothing here: the app runs no gRPC server and no
# asyncio client. grpcio-status moves with it: gRPC releases them in
# lockstep, and 1.85.1 requires grpcio>=1.85.1 and protobuf>=6.33.5,<8.
grpcio==1.85.1
grpcio-status==1.85.1
# pyasn1 arrives only via pyasn1-modules ← google-auth, whose one importer of
# either is google/auth/crypt/_python_rsa.py, the backend for keys of the
# `rsa` package — a google-auth extra, not installed. google-auth parses
# every key and certificate here with cryptography, and importing the
# default app or the portal loads neither pyasn1 nor pyasn1_modules. 0.6.5
# (2026-10-27) fixes four high, quadratic-CPU decoding bugs on
# attacker-supplied ASN.1: CVE-2026-119194 (GHSA-fm9c-5rcf-2q5p, schemaless
# containers), CVE-2026-119195 (GHSA-9m75-vc3g-4x39, constructed strings and
# indefinite-length ANY), CVE-2026-119196 (GHSA-2qm8-x737-gmw5, constructed
# BIT STRING) and CVE-2026-119197 (GHSA-mm57-4v3p-rchx, binary REAL). None
# is reachable: no request's bytes ever reach pyasn1, since Firebase ID and
# App Check tokens are JWTs verified through cryptography. A hygiene bump;
# pyasn1-modules 0.4.2 accepts pyasn1>=0.6.1,<0.7.0.
pyasn1==0.6.5
# PDF text extraction, added 2026-08-26 for the MCP get_document_text tool
# (the stdlib cannot read a PDF). Pure Python — no native libs, so App Engine
# Standard safe. Sole consumer: utils/pdf_text.py. Its caller,
# mcp/handlers.get_document_text, reads the file through
# models/document.get_document_bytes, which refuses one whose recorded
# file_size exceeds DOCUMENT_TEXT_MAX_BYTES BEFORE any byte is downloaded
# (pypdf's parse memory runs ~2-3x file size on ordinary PDFs; a crafted
# page within every cap below still reaches some 200 MB), and pdf_text
# isolates each page's extract_text() so one malformed page never kills the
# call.
# 6.19.0 -> 6.21.4 (2026-11-05) closes eighteen advisories (CVE-2026-113786,
# -113789 to -113795, -115572, -115580, -115585, -119203 to -119209), all but
# one denial of service on a crafted PDF. pdf_text opens a file with
# PdfReader's defaults (strict=False), refuses it if encrypted, counts its
# pages, then runs extract_text() page by page, so ten are REACHABLE through
# any outside PDF filed in a dossier (from the portal through « Verser »,
# uploaded, or through the connector). Nine strike at open or while counting
# pages: a file cut inside its xref table or with no trailer looped forever
# (-113794, -119203); an oversized xref subsection, repeated malformed xref
# entries, a huge xref stream (-115580, -115585, -119209), a page shared by
# many references (-119204), huge arrays or dictionaries (-113790), stacked
# filters (-113795) and repeated references to one missing object (-115572)
# ran long or exhausted memory. The last three strike again under
# extract_text(), as do dense content streams (-113791). The other eight
# need image extraction (-113786), form fields (-113789), page labels
# (-119206), strict mode (-119207), an encrypted file's page count (-119208;
# refused as encrypted first) or pypdf's editing API —
# PdfWriter.find_outline_item (-113792), TreeObject.remove_child (-113793),
# cloning (-119205) — none of which runs here.
# The fixes add caps — 16 filters and 300 MB of decoding work per stream,
# 100_000 items per array or dictionary, 2_000_000 tokens per content
# stream, 2_000_000 xref-stream entries and 2_000_000 copied page-dictionary
# entries per file, no standard xref subsection longer than the file can
# hold — on top of 6.19.0's caps (64-byte object ids, ToUnicode tokens, 256
# /Widths per simple font, FlateDecode recovery stopped at 5 MB), all left
# at their defaults: nothing here overrides pypdf.Configuration or
# PdfReader's xref_stream_maximum_entries. Past a cap in a page's own
# content stream or fonts, the page comes back without text and with
# page_extraction_failed:<n> (so would a real vector page of some 670_000
# joined line segments, or 286_000 stroked one by one); past one in a form
# XObject the page draws, pypdf drops that form's text with a WARNING and the
# page passes unflagged. At open or while counting pages, the file is refused
# invalid_pdf, except past the xref-stream cap in an older (/Prev) section,
# which pypdf drops with a WARNING before opening the rest. The caps bound
# each stream, not the call.
# 6.20.0's text changes come along, and no secure version avoids them. On
# 210 public PDFs (15,423 pages of codes, regulations, judgments and pypdf's
# corpus), Word-produced judgments and LégisQuébec's acts extract
# byte-identical, but wrapped table-cell lines now join with no separator
# (upstream #4132), so tables and forms can glue words — 40 pairs on 14
# pages, 28 of them in LégisQuébec regulations' mandatory forms (P-40.1,
# r. 3: "Loi sur laprotection du consommateur"), others in the Criminal
# Code's tables ("Banc de laReine"). #4124 restored 258 missing word gaps
# (one pdfLaTeX document, counted in its two copies) and removed 14
# spurious spaces, but split three words in C-25.01, r. 0.4's form ("reve
# nu"). A classic-xref file (Word, scanners) also costs one more copy of
# itself per call (#4239).
# Since 2026-10-28 (upstream PR 4280, listed in the 6.21.4 notes) pypdf takes
# pure performance bugs as public issues: the next slow-parse fix may come
# with no advisory, hence no Dependabot alert.
# Since 6.17.0, pypdf logs a WARNING (logger pypdf._cmap) per page for each
# Type1C font without /ToUnicode, fontTools not being installed: noise in
# Cloud Logging, the text unchanged, but formatting the font dictionary was
# the dominant CPU cost on a crafted file. utils/pdf_text.py drops that one
# message with a filter on the pypdf._cmap logger itself (one on the parent
# "pypdf" logger would not apply: logger filters are not inherited). Checked
# at 6.21.4: same logger, same prefix. Re-check its prefix at every pypdf
# bump; tests/test_pdf_text.py fails if pypdf stops logging the notice under
# that logger and prefix.
pypdf==6.21.4
# NOTE: the `setuptools<81` constraint that used to live here is GONE, and
# with it setuptools itself — `opentelemetry-instrumentation` dropped its
# pkg_resources dependency at 0.49b0, so nothing pulls setuptools into the
# graph any more. That is what closes CVE-2026-59890 (PYSEC-2026-3447 /
# GHSA-h35f-9h28-mq5c) on all three scanning surfaces at once, rather than
# suppressing it on each. Do not reintroduce a setuptools pin without
# re-reading why it existed.
